`docs/specs/relational-db-processor.md` is a pre-v6 document, and reading it as current is a trap. Checked against the tree at v6.2.2-dev.74:

| The spec says | The code at this tag |
| --- | --- |
| `onStrands(strands: InternalTransmitterUpdate<TDocument>[])` | **no `onStrands` anywhere**; `IProcessor.onOperations(operations: OperationWithContext[])` in `packages/shared/processors/types.ts:54` |
| `PowerhouseDB<TDatabase> = Kysely<TDatabase>` in `packages/document-drive` | **no `PowerhouseDB`, no `packages/document-drive`**; the base class holds `relationalDb: IRelationalDb<TDatabaseSchema>` |
| `ProcessorFactory = (driveId: string) => ProcessorRecord[]` with a `ListenerFilter` | a `ProcessorFactoryBuilder` receiving the host module, and `ProcessorFilter` |
| migrations discovered automatically by the base class | the generated processor overrides **`initAndUpgrade()`** and calls `up(this.relationalDb)` itself |

The current generated template is the authority:

```ts
export class MyProcessor extends RelationalDbProcessor<DB> {
  onOperations(operations: OperationWithContext[]): Promise<void> { … }
  onDisconnect(): Promise<void> { … }
  static override getNamespace(driveId: string): string { return super.getNamespace(driveId); }
  override async initAndUpgrade(): Promise<void> { await up(this.relationalDb); }
}
```

What survives from the spec is its **intent**, and that intent did ship: a base class that hides database setup and migration wiring so a processor author writes business logic, a factory pattern with filtering, project-level migrations, and type-safe queries. `ph generate processor <name> --processor-type relationalDb` still exists (`oneOf(["analytics", "relationalDb"])`). Treat the spec as design rationale and the template plus `packages/shared/processors/relational/types.ts` as the API.

